 11 │ theorem same (xs: List (Option Nat)): foo xs = bar xs := rfl
    │                                                          ─┬─  
    │                                                           ╰─── `foo xs` and `bar xs` aren't equal
    │ 
    │ Note 1: computing both sides gives `List.foldLeft xs 0 (acc o => (match …) acc o)` and `List.foldLeft xs 0 (acc o => (match …) acc o)`
    │ 
    │ Note 2: `rfl` proves `a = b` only when both sides compute to the same value
    │ 
    │ Note 3: `(match …)` is a `match` written inside `foo` and `bar`, marked "this `match`" above; the compiler turned it into a function that source can't name. The same `match` written again is another such function, equal to it only if neither calls the definition it's written in. To state a lemma about it, make it a local `def` in its arm and refer to it as `owner.name`, where `owner` is the definition it is written in (docs/syntax.md, "Proving things about a definition with an inner `match`")
    │ 
    │ Note 4: two different `(match …)` appear here, which print alike but are different hidden functions; two such functions are equal only when they split the same value and every branch gives the same result, and neither calls the definition it's written in; to prove something about both, name the step as a `def` and use it in both places
    │ 
    │ Note 5: this statement is false; a counterexample:
    │           xs = [None]
    │         `foo xs = bar xs` is false:
    │           left:  0
    │           right: 1
